Yes. If I translate your objective into system terms, the target is not merely “make houses cheaper.” It is:

Make responsible participation in American life reliably convert into housing security, ownership opportunity, and wealth-building—without creating an easy extraction surface for speculators, fraudsters, or subsidy arbitrage.

The current system has several distinct failures. As of Q2 2026, the U.S. homeownership rate was 65.0%, while homeowner vacancy was only 1.2%.  Existing homes sold for a median $434,100 in July, and Freddie Mac’s average 30-year mortgage rate was 6.95% on September 17.  Meanwhile, Harvard’s 2026 housing report says cost burdens remain exceptionally high, particularly for lower-income renters, even as some rental markets have begun softening.  So this is simultaneously a price, financing, supply, mobility, and distribution problem.

A system engineered around the outcome you described could look like this:

* Build far more starter housing, not merely “units.” Townhouses, duplexes, small detached homes, condos, ADUs, manufactured homes and other lower-cost ownership formats need to be economically buildable. Permitting, minimum-lot rules, parking requirements, infrastructure delays and construction costs all affect that supply. HUD itself identifies regulatory constraints and affordable-supply shortages as contributors to cost burdens. 
* Do not juice demand without adding supply. A huge universal down-payment subsidy can partly capitalize into higher prices when houses are scarce. Buyer assistance is structurally stronger when paired with additional construction or attached specifically to new incremental supply.
* Create an ownership on-ramp rather than a cliff. Matched savings, assumable/portable financing where legally and financially workable, shared-equity structures, mortgage insurance, VA/FHA programs, and targeted rate assistance can reduce the enormous difference between being able to afford a monthly payment and being able to cross the initial cash barrier. FHA already plays a large first-time-buyer role, and Ginnie Mae supports substantial VA lending. 
* Protect the starter-home layer from subsidy extraction. Assistance can be tied to genuine owner-occupancy, with occupancy verification, resale rules, prorated subsidy recapture when appropriate, beneficial-owner disclosure, and audit trails. A subsidized starter home immediately flipped into an investor asset would defeat the program’s intended function.
* Separate shelter policy from ownership policy. Emergency shelter and housing stability answer one social problem; subsidized wealth-building answers another. Mixing them produces confused incentives. Ownership assistance can use transparent eligibility requirements—income, owner occupancy, ability to sustain the property, first-time status where relevant—rather than trying to have government decide who is morally “deserving.”
* Reward maintenance of the asset, not merely acquisition. Insurance, property taxes, repairs and utilities increasingly contribute to homeowner burdens.  A household that barely clears closing but cannot replace a roof five years later hasn’t received durable housing security. Reserve accounts, maintenance education and insurance resilience matter.
* Increase mobility. High transaction costs and the loss of old low-rate mortgages can make people stay in houses that no longer fit them. More mobility releases existing inventory. This is one reason financing architecture matters almost as much as construction.
* Preserve several wealth-building ladders. Homeownership is powerful, but the social contract becomes brittle if owning one particular scarce asset is the only practical route to middle-class wealth. Retirement saving, employee ownership, business creation and broad-based investment access reduce the zero-sum political pressure around housing.

Then comes your anti-cheat architecture.

I would model every public benefit as:

intended recipient -> eligibility proof -> benefit -> intended behavior -> measurable outcome

with an adversarial branch:

benefit -> possible arbitrage -> detection -> clawback / exclusion / prosecution where applicable

You don’t need massive surveillance to do that. You need good provenance. For an owner-occupancy benefit, for example: verified identity, property title, financing records, primary-residence certification, a reasonable occupancy period, transparent beneficial ownership, automated anomaly detection, randomized audits, and proportionate clawbacks for material misrepresentation.

And then GoalSeek the American Dream backward.

Suppose the desired state is:

A household that works, saves, behaves responsibly and wants permanence can reasonably acquire a safe home, raise a family there, withstand ordinary setbacks, accumulate equity, and eventually pass something forward.

Work backward:

intergenerational stability
← survivable ownership
← maintenance + insurance + affordable payment
← sustainable mortgage
← attainable purchase price
← abundant appropriate housing
← buildable land + infrastructure + construction capacity
← rules that permit supply

At the same time:

fair access
← benefits reaching intended households
← auditable eligibility
← low-arbitrage program design
← clear rules + provenance + enforcement

That is the daisy chain.

And there is one Pantheon move I think is especially useful here: plot armor for ordinary families.

Not magical guarantees against consequences—real redundancy:

emergency savings + fixed financing + insurance + portable skills + equity + family/community support + recoverable failure

A healthy social contract does not promise that nothing bad happens. It makes ordinary setbacks non-terminal.

Then the American Dream stops being “everyone is guaranteed a mansion.” It becomes something more structurally defensible:

Play by comprehensible rules, contribute, take responsibility, and the system should contain a plausible path toward security, ownership and advancement—and enough resilience that one ordinary piece of bad luck doesn’t erase decades of work.

That is a much more interesting housing target than simply manipulating the median home price.